%% DM26-0670

%% Cutting a release, publishing its images and taking them, as built (2026-11-04, infra/release.nu, infra/images.nu, infra/up.nu, infra/INFRA.md). The three are separate steps. release.nu pushes only to
%% the local registry and never to Docker Hub or to git. No step signs: the tag is annotated, and signing starts at the deployment, with the pipeline's commit and the signed record.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Op as Operator
    participant Rel as infra/release.nu
    participant Img as infra/images.nu
    participant Nix as nix build
    participant LR as Local registry
    participant Git as The checkout
    participant Hub as Docker Hub
    actor Cons as Who takes the release
    participant Up as infra/up.nu
    participant CR as The cluster's registry

    Note over Op,Git: Cut it
    Op->>Rel: release.nu run
    Rel->>Rel: refuse dirty tracked files or a detached HEAD
    Rel->>Rel: the gates: unit, deny, secrets and resolve block, clippy reports.<br/>A failure ends it with nothing changed
    Rel->>Rel: semver.nu's evidence and suggestion. Refuse nothing to release,<br/>a version not after the last, or a tag that exists
    Op->>Rel: the highlights, then y (--dry-run stops here, after a preview)
    Rel->>Git: VERSION, committed as chore(release): prepare vX.Y.Z (resumes if that is HEAD)
    Rel->>Img: images.nu sync --all, the dev lock set aside
    Img->>Nix: build every image
    Nix-->>Img: the archives
    Img->>LR: skopeo copy, recording each digest
    Rel->>Rel: lock problems refuse, then promote: images.lock.nuon, with what it was built from
    Rel->>Rel: the determinism gate
    Rel->>Git: CHANGELOG.md, written by changelog.nu from the evidence and the lock's digests
    Op->>Rel: y
    Rel->>Git: lock and changelog, committed as chore(release): vX.Y.Z.<br/>Refused, and left untagged, if the diff touched anything else
    Rel->>Git: git tag -a vX.Y.Z, then a commit of X.Y.Z+dev
    Rel-->>Op: the git push to run, which it does not run

    Note over Op,Hub: Publish it, by hand. No check here ties this to the tag or the lock
    Op->>Img: images.nu publish --to docker.io/ACCOUNT --arch A --also-tag X.Y.Z
    Img->>Nix: build each nix image for that architecture (a mirror only when named)
    Img->>Hub: skopeo, as polar-IMAGE:TAG-ARCH, for the table's tag and X.Y.Z

    Note over Cons,CR: Take it, from a checkout of the release
    Cons->>Up: up.nu T --import-from docker.io/ACCOUNT --import-tag X.Y.Z
    Up->>Up: every image the target runs: its charts rendered alone, and the catalog's.<br/>--import-tag with no --import-from is refused
    Up->>Img: images.nu import --skip-mirrors --tag X.Y.Z
    Img->>Hub: read polar-IMAGE:X.Y.Z-ARCH, this machine's architecture
    Img->>CR: copy, under its table tag. Each digest goes to the dev lock.<br/>Nothing here compares it with the committed lock
    Up->>Img: sync the mirrors from upstream
    Up->>Up: render, pinning each image by digest: the dev lock over the committed one.<br/>An image the render names that was not prepared stops it
    Note over Cons,Up: --dev builds and pushes instead. With both, import first, then build.<br/>With neither, the step is skipped, and what is in the registry is asked for
